president Trump's threat to deploy  federal troops in US cities in response  to protests over the death of George  Floyd is raising concerns the military  is being politicized former defense  secretary Robert Gates served eight  presidents in various roles including  George W Bush and Barack Obama his new  book is exercise of power American  failures successes and a new path  forward in the post-cold war world it  looks at the leadership and foreign  policy decisions of recent presidents  including President Trump secretary  Gates joins us now good morning  secretary gates thanks for being with us  I want to start with this sort of recent  clash if you will between military  leaders in President Trump  defense secretary mark Esper and other  leaders spoke out against the  president's calls to use the military in  containing the protests what kind of  impact do you think that has when the  military and and and the  commander-in-chief clash like that  publicly well every president that I  worked for and particularly the two that  I worked for a secretary of defense had  their disagreements with the military  and always the president prevails when  when he feels strongly about something I  think that the real issue for the  military leaders in this recent incident  instance was the question of using  Regular Army forces in Washington and  other cities rather than the National  Guard  the truth is Regular Army forces are  drink trained to do one thing and that's  kill our enemies they're not trained in  law enforcement they're not trained in  crowd control the National Guard is and  so when you look at an army uniform you  see very little difference between the  National Guard and Regular Army but  there really is a difference in terms of  their training and and the purposes for  which they are intended so I think that  was the issue that concerned a lot of  the former military leaders you've said  that that every president uses the  military as a prop at some point but  that this president has taken it to a  new level the chairman of the  Chiefs mark Milley apologized for  appearing at a photo op in a park across  from the White House and and I'm  wondering what you thought of that photo  op well I thought it was unfortunate and  and frankly I felt like the military  leadership was being exploited I think  they got themselves into a situation  that they didn't expect and an afterword  regret it and that was the basis for  general Millie's apology I think he was  very sensitive to the appearance of the  American military being political and  one of the fundamental tenets for our  men and women in uniform is being  apolitical and serving all of the  American people not just one president  or one party or another you and other  military leaders have voiced support for  changing the names of military bases  that are they're named in honor of  Confederate generals the president is  does not support that idea at least at  this point why do you think first of all  why do you think those bases have held  those names for so long and why do you  think it's right to change them now I  can't answer the reason why-y-y they've  lasted so long I think hardly it's just  inertia partly the the names do have  some meaning to to the troops who were  trained there or served there but I  think the time has come and I think the  events of the last couple of weeks and  the killing of George Floyd and and the  demonstrations have presented us with an  opportunity to make a change where you  know when we have these bases named for  Confederate generals it suggests we're  celebrating them and my view is we can't  rewrite history but we need to  understand it we need to remember it but  we don't need to celebrate it and I  think that's why the time has come to  change the names of these military  facilities I mean it's always puzzled me  why we don't have a Fort George  Washington or  for patent or or or a facility name for  an african-american Medal of Honor  recipient so I think it's an opportunity  really to to make the change at this  point as you've pointed out the military  in many ways for a long time led to  integration in this country but general  Charles Brown jr. just became the first  African American to lead to lead one of  the nation's military services as chief  of staff of the Air Force why do you why  do you think that took so long given the  history of integration in the military  well it clearly took too long we have  had African Americans in senior  leadership positions Colin Powell's  chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff  General Lloyd Austin was the commander  in Iraq when I was secretary but but the  truth is that at the four-star rank  there are too few and too far between  and I think a general Milley is has  turned his attention to that and the  attention of the other chiefs in terms  of the promotion processes to make sure  that that more African Americans are and  other minorities are promoted into the  most senior leadership positions in the  Pentagon it's it's it's really it's not  in I mean they will be qualified there's  no question in my mind about that but  there just needs to be more attention  paid to make sure making sure that  minority leaders have the opportunity to  be in the most senior positions do you  think there's been a systemic problem I  think so and probably in all of the  services I think the recent events  frankly have have I think been a turning  point you know in the attitudes of a lot  of people and I think it's really  grabbed the attention of the senior  military leadership as well we the  military as you say was out in front in  terms of integration and providing  opportunities for African Americans long  before the rest of the society but but  in particularly in terms of promoting  leaders to the most senior positions and  frankly  some thread of racism there still needs  to be attention paid to this in the  military and and as I would say more  attention as we go forward you say  they've been a turning point for a lot  of people mr. secretary have they been a  turning point for you in some way I  think so I I think that you know I think  what many of us missed and and I feel  like I've been an inclusive leader in  the in the organizations that I've led  but I don't think many of us fully  appreciated the day-to-day kind of  humiliations and and and difficulties  that African Americans even the most  successful African Americans have had to  deal with and I think I think that's  really come to the fore in the last two  or three weeks in a way for many of us  that had not been particularly evident  before that all right secretary gates  thank you so much for being with us this  morning exercise of power is on sale  today and we will be right back
 

